    "people": [
      {
        "firstName": "Baptiste",
        "lastName": "MOREL",
        "role": "professor",
        "year": "",
        "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/baptistemorel",
        "photo": "baptiste-morel.jpg",
        "background": "Organism",
        "biosketch": "Dr Baptiste Morel is Associate Professor at University Savoie Mont-Blanc (Chambéry), working in the Inter-University Laboratory of Human Movement Biology (LIBM). His research investigates fatigue phenomenon at the interface of muscle physiology and biomechanics with applications in the domains of health, sports performance, and ecology. In particular, he develops a conceptual framework and a mathematical model of the force-velocity-endurance relationship. In this context, he is Principal Investigator of the interdisciplinary projects BIOCARE FActory (building a biopsychosocial model of cancer related fatigue) and FOVEAL (ANR - Force-velocity-endurance in animal locomotion). He also collaborates with several professional sports structures (LOU Rugby, AG2R La Mondiale) and the french cycling and rowing federations within a French national agency grant project for the Paris 2024 Olympics (THPCA2024)."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "institution": "École Normale Supérieure de Lyon",
    "city": "Lyon",
    "lat": 45.74906,
    "lng": 4.84789,
    "country": "FR",
    "people": [
      {
        "firstName": "Jonathan",
        "lastName": "ENRIQUEZ",
        "role": "professor",
        "year": "",
        "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/jonathan-enriquez-471a7bab/",
        "photo": "jonathan-enriquez.png",
        "background": "Cell",
        "biosketch": "Prof. Jonathan Enriquez is group leader at Institute of Functional Genomics of Lyon (IGFL) at the Ecole Normal Supérieure of Lyon (ENSL), France. From 2010 to 2015 Dr Enriquez was postdoctoral Research Scientist at Columbia University, New York. In 2016, he obtained a permanent position at CNRS in France and started his own group in 2017 at IGFL. He uses Drosophila melanogaster to discover new biological concepts and mechanisms of development. His research combines genetics, molecular biology, imaging, post-imaging processing, computational biology, and behavioral approaches."
      },
      {
        "firstName": "Eglantine",
        "lastName": "HEUDE",
        "role": "professor",
        "year": "",
        "linkedin": "",
        "photo": "eglantine-heude.jpg",
        "background": "Cell",
        "biosketch": "Prof. Eglantine Heude is a permanent CNRS researcher expert in Developmental and Evolutionary Biology. She has just created her team at the “Institut de Génomique Fonctionnelle de Lyon” (ENS de Lyon, September 2024) to develop her own research thematic investigating the developmental and evolutionary origins of tetrapods. Since her PhD, she has focused on the cellular and molecular mechanisms at the origin of a functional and integrated musculoskeletal system in vertebrates. The current research axes combine functional genomics, transcriptomics, comparative embryology and comparative anatomy approaches by studying different conventional and unconventional vertebrate models. Her team seeks to elucidate the morphogenetic bases of morphological innovations and musculoskeletal adaptions to new environments. Eglantine Heude has published 22 papers (citations > 800, H index 17). She is financially supported by the French National Research Agency for young researchers (ANR JCJC)."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "institution": "King's College London",
    "city": "London",
    "lat": 51.50853,
    "lng": -0.12574,
    "country": "GB",
    "people": [
      {
        "firstName": "Cameron",
        "lastName": "HILL",
        "role": "professor",
        "year": "",
        "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/cameron-hill-0933a668/",
        "photo": "cameron-hill.jpg",
        "background": "Tissue",
        "biosketch": "Dr Cameron Hill is a postdoctoral research associate at King’s College London, UK, within the Randall Centre for Cell and Molecular Biophysics. Prior to this role, he worked as a research technician in the Structure and Motion Laboratory at the Royal Veterinary College, UK, (2017–2019), where he employed kinematic video analysis, X-ray Reconstruction of Moving Morphology (XROMM), and musculoskeletal robotics to investigate anuran locomotion. Cameron holds a BSc in Sport and Exercise Science from Coventry University, UK, (2011–2014) and completed his PhD at the same institution (2014–2018), examining the effects of ageing and obesity on isometric force, work loop power output, and fatigue resistance of isolated locomotor and respiratory skeletal muscle. His current research uses synchrotron-based, time-resolved small-angle X-ray diffraction to probe the regulatory states of myosin-containing thick filaments and actin-containing thin filaments in skeletal muscle during contraction. This work focuses on phenotypic differences between fast- and slow-twitch muscle across a range of contractile paradigms, including twitch and tetanic contraction, unloaded shortening, active stretch, length-step perturbations, and force–velocity profiling. He is also interested in how disease models, including ageing and obesity, impair contractile function at the cellular level, and in developing strategies to restore muscle performance."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
