      {
        "firstName": "Laurence",
        "lastName": "VICO",
        "role": "professor",
        "year": "",
        "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/laurence-vico-b169391b/",
        "photo": "laurence-vico.jpg",
        "background": "Organism",
        "biosketch": "Prof. Laurence VICO is a physiologist, Research Director at INSERM, working at Jean Monnet University in Saint-Etienne. She leads the SAINBIOSE Unit (INSERM U1059) which includes two teams, one working on cardiovascular diseases, the other on osteoarticular diseases. Her research themes on the skeletal system encompasses the understanding of the mechanical and metabolic deconditioning occurring in extreme (spaceflight and analogs) or disuse (osteoporosis, inactivity, nutrition disorders) conditions. Such (dys)adaptations encompass intertwined loops leading to bone loss, vascular changes, muscle shift and metabolic alterations. Another target is the effects of pharmaceutical or physical training strategies and their safe and efficient application to frail subjects. Laurence Vico has published more than 300 papers (citations > 8,000, H index 53) and is financially supported by the ANR, the European Agency or the Centre National des Études Spatiales."
      },
      {
        "firstName": "Damien",
        "lastName": "FREYSSENET",
        "role": "professor",
        "year": "",
        "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/damien-freyssenet-79218089/",
        "photo": "damien-freyssenet.png",
        "background": "Tissue",
        "biosketch": "Prof. Damien Freyssenet is an exercise physiologist and biochemist, Professor at Jean Monnet University in Saint-Etienne. He is currently head of the Deconditioning Reconditioning of skeletal Muscle - Systemic Environment research team at the Laboratoire Interuniversitaire de Biologie de la Motricité. Damien Freyssenet's research activity focuses on the mechanisms of skeletal muscle deconditioning under physiological (dry immersion) and pathological (cancer, myopathies, sepsis) conditions, as well as on the development of strategies to limit/prevent this deconditioning. Damien Freyssenet has published 61 papers (citations > 6,000, H index 28). Damien Freyssenet is financially supported by the Association Française contre les Myopathies, the Fondation ARC pour la Recherche sur le Cancer, the Ligue contre le Cancer, or the Centre National des Études Spatiales."
      },
